      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Number: R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02 revision 1\n- Scheme: The cert number starts with \"R49-1/2006\" — this is a basic OIML certificate (scheme A is typically for MAA certificates; standard certificates... Actually the schema says scheme (A|B). Regular OIML-CS certificates are scheme A? Actually in OIML-CS, Scheme A is the basic OIML Certificate of Conformity and Scheme B is the MAA certificate. This one doesn't mention MAA, so scheme A.\n- Project number: SO13202564\n- Page total: 5\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 16 July 2013 (revision 1 date)\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, the Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Itron, Eau et Energie Thermique, 11 Boulevard Pasteur, 67500 Haguenau, France\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Itron, same address\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (the schema examples say \"Gas meter\", so \"Water meter\" fits)\n- Type designations: [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\"]\n- Description: \"Water meter intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n\n**Model family:**\nThis cert covers multiple meter sizes: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300. And within each DN size, there may be two variants (columns with different Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4 combos, corresponding to ratio Q3/Q1 = 160 or 250 or 400).\n\nLet me carefully parse the tables.\n\n**Page 3, Table 1 (Class 1, DN65–DN125):**\n- DN65: Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, Ratio=250, DN=65\n- DN80 (col 1): Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=100, Q4=125, Ratio=160, DN=80\n- DN80 (col 2): Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, Q3=160, Q4=200, Ratio=250, DN=80\n- DN100 (col 1): Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, Ratio=160, DN=100\n- DN100 (col 2): Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Ratio=250, DN=100\n\nWait, DN100 col2: Q1=1,0; Q2=1,6; Q3=250; Q4=312,5; ratio 250. Hmm, Q3/Q1 = 250/1 = 250. Yes.\n\n- DN125 (col 1): Q1=1.56, Q2=2.50, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Ratio=160, DN=125\n- DN125 (col 2): Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500, Ratio=250, DN=125\n\nAccuracy class: 1 (whole table)\nMax admissible pressure: 16 bar (whole table)\nVerification scale interval: 0.0001 m³ for first 3 columns (DN65, DN80×2), 0.001 for remaining 4 (DN100×2, DN125×2)\n\n**Page 3, Table 2 (Class 1, DN150–DN300):**\n- DN150 (col 1): Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=400, Q4=500, Ratio=160, DN=150\n- DN150 (col 2): Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Ratio=250, DN=150\n- DN200: Q1=3.94, Q2=6.30, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Ratio=160, DN=200\n- DN250: Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, Ratio=160, DN=250\n- DN300: Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, Ratio=160, DN=300\n\nAccuracy class 1. Max pressure: 16 bar for first 3 columns (DN150×2, DN200), 10 bar for DN250, DN300. Verification scale interval: 0.001 for first 4 (DN150×2, DN200, DN250), 0.01 for DN300.\n\n**Page 4, Table 1 (Class 2, DN25–DN65):**\n- DN25 (col 1): Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, Ratio=400, DN=25\n- DN25 (col 2): Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, Ratio=400, DN=25\n- DN40 (col 1): Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.100, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, Ratio=400, DN=40\n- DN40 (col 2): Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, Ratio=400, DN=40\n- DN50 (col 1): Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, Ratio=400, DN=50\n- DN50 (col 2): Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, Ratio=400, DN=50\n- DN65 (col 1): Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.250, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, Ratio=400, DN=65\n- DN65 (col 2): Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400, Q3=100, Q4=125, Ratio=400, DN=65\n\nAccuracy class 2, max pressure 16 bar.\nVerification scale interval: 0.00001 for first 2 (DN25×2), 0.0001 for the rest.\n\n**Page 4, Table 2 (Class 2, DN80–DN150):**\n- DN80 (col 1): Q1=0.25, Q2=0.40, Q3=100, Q4=125, Ratio=400, DN=80\n- DN80 (col 2): Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, Ratio=400, DN=80\n- DN100 (col 1): Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, Ratio=400, DN=100\n- DN100 (col 2): Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Ratio=400, DN=100\n- DN125 (col 1): Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=500, Ratio=400, DN=125\n\nWait, Q3=250, Q4=500 for DN125 col 1? Let me re-read: `<tr><td>Overload flow rate Q4 (m³/h)</td><td>125</td><td>200</td><td>200</td><td>312,5</td><td>312,5</td><td>500</td><td>500</td><td>787,5</td></tr>`. So col1=125 (DN80a), col2=200 (DN80b), col3=200 (DN100a), col4=312.5 (DN100b), col5=312.5 (DN125a), col6=500 (DN125b), col7=500 (DN150a), col8=787.5 (DN150b).\n\nAnd Q3 row: 100, 160, 160, 250, 250, 400, 400, 630.\n- DN80a: Q3=100, Q4=125 (ratio 400: 100/0.25 = 400 ✓)\n- DN80b: Q3=160, Q4=200 (160/0.4 = 400 ✓)\n- DN100a: Q3=160, Q4=200 (160/0.4 = 400 ✓)\n- DN100b: Q3=250, Q4=312.5 (250/0.625 = 400 ✓)\n- DN125a: Q3=250, Q4=312.5 (250/0.625 = 400 ✓)\n- DN125b: Q3=400, Q4=500 (400/1.00 = 400 ✓)\n- DN150a: Q3=400, Q4=500 (400/1.00 = 400 ✓)\n- DN150b: Q3=630, Q4=787.5 (630/1.575 = 400 ✓)\n\nAccuracy class 2, max pressure 16 bar. Verification scale interval: 0.0001 for first 2 (DN80×2), 0.001 for rest.\n\n**Page 5, Table (Class 2, DN200–DN300):**\n- DN200: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Ratio=400, DN=200\n- DN250: Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, Ratio=400, DN=250\n- DN300: Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, Ratio=400, DN=300\n\nAccuracy class 2. Max pressure: 16 for DN200, 10 for DN250+DN300. Verification scale interval: 0.001 for DN200, DN250; 0.01 for DN300.\n\nSo the total number of model variants:\n- Class 1: DN65(1) + DN80(2) + DN100(2) + DN125(2) + DN150(2) + DN200(1) + DN250(1) + DN300(1) = 12\n- Class 2: DN25(2) + DN40(2) + DN50(2) + DN65(2) + DN80(2) + DN100(2) + DN125(2) + DN150(2) + DN200(1) + DN250(1) + DN300(1) = 19\n\nTotal = 31 variants.\n\nHow to model this? The schema allows:\n- model_family.models[] with model_id, label, attributes\n- characteristics.model_level[] attribute × model matrix\n- matrix_tables[] for preserving the tables\n\nI think the best approach:\n1. Create `matrix_tables` entries for each of the 5 tables (class 1 DN65-125, class 1 DN150-300, class 2 DN25-65, class 2 DN80-150, class 2 DN200-300).\n2. Create `model_family.models[]` with each variant identified by DN size + Q3 (or flow ratio) to distinguish. E.g., \"DN80-Q3=100\" vs \"DN80-Q3=160\".\n3. Create `characteristics.model_level[]` entries for the varying attributes: minimum_flow_rate_q1, transitional_flow_rate_q2, permanent_flow_rate_q3, overload_flow_rate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, nominal_diameter, accuracy_class, maximum_admissible_pressure, verification_scale_interval.\n\nActually, accuracy_class varies by model (class 1 for one group, class 2 for the other). Max admissible pressure varies too (16 vs 10). Verification scale interval varies per model.\n\nHmm, but putting all 31 models into model_level values arrays for 9 attributes would be very verbose. That's what the schema asks for though. Alternatively, use the models' `attributes` dict to hold per-model attributes and keep model_level for the key flow rates. Let me think about the balance.\n\nThe schema example for R137 shows both matrix_tables AND model_level. I'll do:\n\n- model_family.family_name: \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\" — hmm, family_name is \"explicit family name if any, else null\". The type is \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\", which serves as the family. I could set family_name to \"Sharpflow\" or null. The models are designated by meter size and flow-rate configuration. I'll set family_name: \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\".\n\n- models[]: one entry per variant. model_id could be \"DN25-Q3=10-R400\" style. Let me define a consistent scheme: `DN<size>_<class><Q3 value>`. Simpler: model_id \"DN25 Q3=10 m³/h class 2\". Hmm.\n\nLet me use model_id like \"DN25/Q3=10\" with attributes {accuracy_class: 2, permanent_flow_rate_q3: 10, ...}. Actually the label can help: \"DN25, Q3=10 m³/h, ratio 400, class 2\".\n\nAlternatively keep model_id simple: e.g. \"DN25-10\" (DN size + Q3). But class 1 and class 2 tables have overlapping DN sizes with different flows: DN65 class 1 has Q3=100 ratio 250; DN65 class 2 col1 has Q3=63, col2 has Q3=100 ratio 400. So DN65+Q3=100 appears in both class 1 (ratio 250) and class 2 (ratio 400). So model_id must include class or ratio. I'll include accuracy class in the model_id.\n\nModel ID format: \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\" etc. Or more readable: \"DN65 · class 1 · Q3=100 m³/h\". Let me go with something like:\n\n- model_id: \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\"\n- label: \"DN25, Q3=10 m³/h, Q3/Q1=400, accuracy class 2\"\n\nLet me enumerate all 31 variants:\n\n**Class 1:**\n1. DN65-class1-Q3=100: Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio=250, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n2. DN80-class1-Q3=100: Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio=160, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n3. DN80-class1-Q3=160: Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio=250, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n4. DN100-class1-Q3=160: Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio=160, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n5. DN100-class1-Q3=250: Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio=250, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n6. DN125-class1-Q3=250: Q1=1.56, Q2=2.50, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio=160, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n7. DN125-class1-Q3=400: Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio=250, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n8. DN150-class1-Q3=400: Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio=160, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n9. DN150-class1-Q3=630: Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio=250, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n10. DN200-class1-Q3=630: Q1=3.94, Q2=6.30, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio=160, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n11. DN250-class1-Q3=1000: Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio=160, pmax=10, VSI=0.001\n12. DN300-class1-Q3=1600: Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio=160, pmax=10, VSI=0.01\n\n**Class 2:**\n13. DN25-class2-Q3=10: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.00001\n14. DN25-class2-Q3=16: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.00001\n15. DN40-class2-Q3=25: Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.100, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n16. DN40-class2-Q3=40: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n17. DN50-class2-Q3=40: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n18. DN50-class2-Q3=63: Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n19. DN65-class2-Q3=63: Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.250, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n20. DN65-class2-Q3=100: Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n21. DN80-class2-Q3=100: Q1=0.25, Q2=0.40, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n22. DN80-class2-Q3=160: Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.0001\n23. DN100-class2-Q3=160: Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n24. DN100-class2-Q3=250: Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n25. DN125-class2-Q3=250: Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n26. DN125-class2-Q3=400: Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n27. DN150-class2-Q3=400: Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n28. DN150-class2-Q3=630: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n29. DN200-class2-Q3=630: Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio=400, pmax=16, VSI=0.001\n30. DN250-class2-Q3=1000: Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio=400, pmax=10, VSI=0.001\n31. DN300-class2-Q3=1600: Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio=400, pmax=10, VSI=0.01\n\nThat's 31 models.\n\nNow for characteristics.model_level: I'll include the main varying attributes. That's a lot of arrays. Given the volume, maybe I should put per-model attributes in each model's `attributes{}` and include model_level for the key flow rates. The schema example (R137) populates model_level with qmax/qt/qmin AND matrix_tables with full rows. Following that pattern, I should populate model_level with q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, nominal_diameter, accuracy_class, maximum_admissible_pressure, verification_scale_interval. That's 9 attributes × 31 values each = 279 value entries. That's big but doable. Alternatively I can put accuracy class etc. only in model attributes and matrix tables.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most useful and compliant. The schema says model_level is \"attribute × model matrix\". The R137 example shows model_level with only qmax, qt, qmin (3 of the 6 columns), while matrix_tables has all columns. So it's acceptable to have model_level cover the primary attributes and let matrix_tables preserve the rest.\n\nGiven output size constraints, I'll:\n- Put full detail in matrix_tables (5 tables).\n- Populate model_family.models[] with model_id, label, and attributes (all per-model attributes as structured values or simple values — the schema shows `attributes{}` as a free dict; I'll use the structured value form to be safe... Actually the R137 example doesn't populate attributes at all. I'll use simple structured objects: {\"value\": x, \"unit_symbol\": \"...\"}).\n\nHmm, but strict rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers shape. That applies to `characteristics`. For model attributes{} it's not explicitly \"characteristics\", but to be safe I'll use the structured form there too. That will make the output huge: 31 models × ~9 attributes × 4 keys each. Let me estimate: each attribute entry ~ 60 chars → 31×9×60 ≈ 17k chars. Plus model_level 9 attributes × 31 values × ~40 chars ≈ 11k. Plus matrix tables 5 × 8 rows × ~9 fields ≈ 8k. Total ~40k chars. That's large but acceptable? Maybe trim.\n\nAlternative: keep models[] entries with attributes as simple {attribute: value, \"unit\": ...} — compact. But risk of violating rule 2? Rule 2 says \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object...\" in context of `characteristics`. model attributes{} is `attributes{}` free-form. The R137 example leaves attributes empty. I think a reasonable middle ground: put per-model attributes in model_level characteristics (as the R137 example does) and keep models[] minimal with model_id and label. And matrix_tables preserves the full tables. That avoids duplication in three places.\n\nSo plan:\n- model_family: family_name \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\", models: 31 entries with model_id + label.\n- characteristics.type_level: the whole-cert attributes: min/max admissible temperature, indicating range, orientation, environmental class, power supply type, battery voltage, software versions.\n- characteristics.model_level: attribute × model for: minimum_flow_rate_q1, transitional_flow_rate_q2, permanent_flow_rate_q3, overload_flow_rate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, nominal_diameter, accuracy_class, maximum_admissible_pressure, verification_scale_interval.\n\nThat's the full matrix. It's big but complete. Hmm, 9 attributes × 31 models. Let me reconsider — is nominal_diameter needed given it's in the model_id (DN size)? I could skip it since DN encodes it. Accuracy class could arguably be a model attribute. Max pressure and VSI vary per model too.\n\nTo keep the output manageable while following the R137 precedent (which only extracted the 3 primary flow attributes into model_level), I'll include model_level for: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, verification_scale_interval, maximum_admissible_pressure, accuracy_class. Skip nominal_diameter (encoded in model_id DN). That's 8 × 31 = 248 entries. Still large.\n\nAlternatively, put accuracy_class etc. at model level via model attributes with simple values. Hmm.\n\nLet me just go with the full model_level arrays; it's the schema-correct representation. I'll be careful with JSON syntax.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider model_id design. Using \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\" as model_id. In model_level values, each entry is {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": ...}. To reduce repetition I must still write full model ids.\n\nLet me now define the type-level characteristics:\n\n- min_max_admissible_temperature: 0,1/50 °C → {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. Wait — \"Min/max admissible temperature (°C): 0,1/50\". Hmm, that's odd — min 0.1°C? For cold potable water meters, typical is 0.1/50 per R49 (MAT 0.1°C to 30°C for cold... actually cold water meter T range 0.1–30, hot 0.1–90). \"0,1/50\" likely means 0.1 to 50 °C. So {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol \"°C\".\n- indicating_range: \"99.999.999\" m³ → this is a formatted number 99999999 (European thousands separator with dots). Value: 99999999, unit \"m³\". As a string \"99.999.999\"? The rule says numbers not strings; thousands dots → 99999999. I'll use 99999999 with unit m³. Hmm, but is \"indicating range 99999999 m³\" sensible? It's the max indicated value. Yes, 8-digit register. I'll record value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\".\n- orientation: \"All positions\" → string value \"All positions\", unit null.\n- environmental_class: \"C\" → value \"C\".\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\" → value \"Battery\".\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V → value 3.6, unit \"V\". Label: battery_voltage_ubattery → canonical: battery_voltage. The markdown: \"U_battery\". Canonical label \"battery_voltage\".\n- software_versions: list \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_; 4.2.5_\". These are software version strings with trailing underscore. Value could be a string \"4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_\". Since it's a list of identifiers, keep as a single string. Or array? Schema wants value: number|{min,max}|string. So single string.\n\nType designations: The type is \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\". Components? \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\" — SWB7 is likely the meter sensor body and CWB7 the calculating/indicating device (converter). The cert says \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\". The type designation combines \"SWB7 + CWB7\". Should I record components? The cert doesn't explicitly name them as separate components with roles; it just names the type. I could add components: meter SWB7 (measuring sensor?) and CWB7 (calculating/indicating device) — but that's speculative. The schema says include components ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies. The type name \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\" does name two designations joined by \"+\", and the description mentions an electronic calculating/indicating device. I think it's reasonable but borderline. I'll record type_designations: [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\"] and skip components to avoid fabrication. Hmm... Actually \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\" — Itron's Sharpflow: SWB7 is the electromagnetic water meter, CWB7... I'm not certain of roles. I'll skip components.\n\n**Recommendation:**\nThe cert references R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006. The certificate number is R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02. Canonical id: \"R49\". Edition: 2006. But there are three parts. The schema has one recommendation object with id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. I could set id \"R49\" and note parts? Hmm. The certificate covers R49-1 (metrological requirements), R49-2 (test methods), R49-3 (test report format). The primary recommendation for conformity is R49 (Water meters). I'll use id \"R49\", edition 2006, and maybe mention parts... The schema doesn't have a parts field. I could use id \"R49\" with edition 2006. Accuracy classes: 1 and 2 (from the tables; also \"class 1 and 2\" in the description).\n\nAlternatively id \"R49-1\"? The certificate number is R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02, and page 2 test reports are R49-1/2006-NL1-*. Hmm, interesting: test report numbers R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01 etc. The certificate seems issued under R49-1 (part 1). But the cert attests conformity with R49-1, R49-2, R49-3. I'll use id \"R49\" edition 2006, and record the parts via... hmm, maybe include \"amendment\": null. I think id \"R49\" is the canonical recommendation. accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"] or [1, 2]? Schema says accuracy_classes[] — I'll use [\"1\",\"2\"]. Hmm, could also list [\"R49-1/2006 (E)\", ...]. Let me keep it simple: id \"R49\", edition 2006.\n\nHmm wait, but should I include three recommendation entries? The schema shows single `recommendation:` object. I'll do one with id \"R49\". Actually, maybe better: id \"R49-1\" since the cert number references R49-1/2006 and conformity primarily per part 1? The cert attests conformity \"with the requirements of the following Recommendation\": lists all three parts. I'll go with id \"R49\", edition 2006. To capture parts, I could add them in... there's no field. Fine.\n\n**Test reports:**\nFour test reports:\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, 41 pages, 14 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, 40 pages, 4 annexes\n- R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01 (note typo: dot instead of slash), 40 pages, 3 annexes\n\nrole: type_evaluation_report. No dates given. Pages: 41, 40, 40, 40. I might note annexes in... schema fields: id, date, pages, role. No annex field; skip annexes (don't fabricate fields, but could I add? Schema says don't fabricate; stick to schema fields). Keep pages only.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Revision \"Initial\", date 22 November 2011, changes \"-\" → changes: null or \"-\"? Empty → null. Use null.\n- Revision \"1\", date 16 July 2013, changes \"New software version and meter sizes added\".\n\n**Footnotes:** none in this cert. Omit.\n\n**Certificate number:** \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02\" with revision 1. Should number include \"revision 1\"? The number field: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02 revision 1\". I'll put number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02\" and revision_history captures revision 1. There's no dedicated revision field at cert level; maybe number should include \"revision 1\"? Hmm. The revision history table covers it. I'll keep number clean: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02\". Actually, to be safe, maybe include the revision in number as printed: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02 revision 1\"? The instruction: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll set number \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02\" and date_issued \"2013-07-16\". Hmm date format — I'll use ISO \"2013-07-16\".\n\nScheme: \"A\" (basic OIML certificate, not MAA).\n\nPage total: 5.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"the Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"C. Oosterman\"\n- person_title: \"Head Certification Board\"\n- phone: \"+31 78 6332332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\n**Applicants:**\n- name: \"Itron\", address_lines: [\"Eau et Energie Thermique\", \"11 Boulevard Pasteur\", \"67500 Haguenau\", \"France\"]. Hmm, \"Eau et Energie Thermique\" is a division name, not an address line per se, but it's part of the printed address block. I'll include it as first address line.\n\n**Manufacturers:** same as applicant.\n\n**certified_type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\"]\n- module_designation: null — the schema example for load cells: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. For a water meter, not applicable. I'll use \"Not applicable\"? Hmm, module_designation is an OIML concept mostly in R60/load cells and modular instruments (R49 has modules too). The cert doesn't mention modules. Per rule 8 (don't fabricate), use null or omit. The schema enum includes \"Not applicable\". I'll omit it... Actually setting \"Not applicable\" when the cert doesn't discuss modules is also fabrication-ish. I'll omit.\n\nHmm, wait: the cert says \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\" — that's an integral part, not separately approved modules. Omit module_designation.\n\n- description: \"Water meter intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n\nAlso page 1 description: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model 'Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7', class 1 and 2.\" I'll use the page-2 fuller description.\n\n**matrix_tables:** 5 tables. Columns: meter_size (well, each column = variant). These tables are transposed: rows are attributes, columns are variants. For matrix_tables, I'll present rows per variant:\n\nTable 1 \"Metrological characteristics, accuracy class 1, DN65–DN125\":\nrows: [{\"meter_size\":\"DN65\",\"q1\":0.40,\"q2\":0.64,\"q3\":100,\"q4\":125,\"ratio_q3_q1\":250,\"nominal_diameter_mm\":65,\"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\":16,\"verification_scale_interval_m3\":0.0001}, ...]\n\nHmm, column naming: use q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, nominal_diameter, max_admissible_pressure, accuracy_class, verification_scale_interval. And column_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"mm\", \"bar\", \"m³\"].\n\nThe schema example shows \"columns\" and \"column_units\" (in example), \"rows\". The YAML schema shows columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]. The R137 example JSON includes column_units. I'll include column_units.\n\nNow, for the tables, note that DN80 has two columns (two variants). In rows per variant, I'll have two rows with meter_size \"DN80\" distinguished by q3. Good.\n\nNow the model_level characteristic arrays. Attributes:\n\n1. minimum_flow_rate_q1, unit \"m³/h\"\n2. transitional_flow_rate_q2, unit \"m³/h\"\n3. permanent_flow_rate_q3, unit \"m³/h\"\n4. overload_flow_rate_q4, unit \"m³/h\"\n5. ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n6. accuracy_class (dimensionless)\n7. maximum_admissible_pressure, unit \"bar\"\n8. verification_scale_interval, unit \"m³\"\n\nAnd nominal_diameter in mm — skip (in model_id). Hmm, actually let me include it for completeness? That would be 9 arrays. The model_id \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\" already encodes DN. I'll skip nominal_diameter in model_level but include in matrix tables rows.\n\nLet me now decide model_id format precisely. \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\". For label: \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7, DN65, accuracy class 1, Q3=100 m³/h\".\n\nLet me write out all model entries:\n\nClass 1 (12):\n1. {\"model_id\": \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\", \"label\": \"DN65, class 1, Q3=100 m³/h\"}\n2. {\"model_id\": \"DN80-class1-Q3=100\", \"label\": \"DN80, class 1, Q3=100 m³/h\"}\n3. {\"model_id\": \"DN80-class1-Q3=160\", \"label\": \"DN80, class 1, Q3=160 m³/h\"}\n4. {\"model_id\": \"DN100-class1-Q3=160\", \"label\": \"DN100, class 1, Q3=160 m³/h\"}\n5. {\"model_id\": \"DN100-class1-Q3=250\", \"label\": \"DN100, class 1, Q3=250 m³/h\"}\n6. {\"model_id\": \"DN125-class1-Q3=250\", \"label\": \"DN125, class 1, Q3=250 m³/h\"}\n7. {\"model_id\": \"DN125-class1-Q3=400\", \"label\": \"DN125, class 1, Q3=400 m³/h\"}\n8. {\"model_id\": \"DN150-class1-Q3=400\", \"label\": \"DN150, class 1, Q3=400 m³/h\"}\n9. {\"model_id\": \"DN150-class1-Q3=630\", \"label\": \"DN150, class 1, Q3=630 m³/h\"}\n10. {\"model_id\": \"DN200-class1-Q3=630\", \"label\": \"DN200, class 1, Q3=630 m³/h\"}\n11. {\"model_id\": \"DN250-class1-Q3=1000\", \"label\": \"DN250, class 1, Q3=1000 m³/h\"}\n12. {\"model_id\": \"DN300-class1-Q3=1600\", \"label\": \"DN300, class 1, Q3=1600 m³/h\"}\n\nClass 2 (19):\n13. {\"model_id\": \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\", ...}\n14. {\"model_id\": \"DN25-class2-Q3=16\", ...}\n15. {\"model_id\": \"DN40-class2-Q3=25\", ...}\n16. {\"model_id\": \"DN40-class2-Q3=40\", ...}\n17. {\"model_id\": \"DN50-class2-Q3=40\", ...}\n18. {\"model_id\": \"DN50-class2-Q3=63\", ...}\n19. {\"model_id\": \"DN65-class2-Q3=63\", ...}\n20. {\"model_id\": \"DN65-class2-Q3=100\", ...}\n21. {\"model_id\": \"DN80-class2-Q3=100\", ...}\n22. {\"model_id\": \"DN80-class2-Q3=160\", ...}\n23. {\"model_id\": \"DN100-class2-Q3=160\", ...}\n24. {\"model_id\": \"DN100-class2-Q3=250\", ...}\n25. {\"model_id\": \"DN125-class2-Q3=250\", ...}\n26. {\"model_id\": \"DN125-class2-Q3=400\", ...}\n27. {\"model_id\": \"DN150-class2-Q3=400\", ...}\n28. {\"model_id\": \"DN150-class2-Q3=630\", ...}\n29. {\"model_id\": \"DN200-class2-Q3=630\", ...}\n30. {\"model_id\": \"DN250-class2-Q3=1000\", ...}\n31. {\"model_id\": \"DN300-class2-Q3=1600\", ...}\n\nNote: DN65 appears in both class 1 (Q3=100, ratio 250) and class 2 (Q3=63 and Q3=100, ratio 400) — distinct model_ids. Good.\n\nNow model_level values per attribute. Order models as listed above.\n\n**minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h):**\n- DN65-c1: 0.40\n- DN80-c1-Q3=100: 0.625\n- DN80-c1-Q3=160: 0.64\n- DN100-c1-Q3=160: 1.0\n- DN100-c1-Q3=250: 1.0\n- DN125-c1-Q3=250: 1.56\n- DN125-c1-Q3=400: 1.60\n- DN150-c1-Q3=400: 2.50\n- DN150-c1-Q3=630: 2.52\n- DN200-c1-Q3=630: 3.94\n- DN250-c1-Q3=1000: 6.25\n- DN300-c1-Q3=1600: 10\n- DN25-c2-Q3=10: 0.025\n- DN25-c2-Q3=16: 0.040\n- DN40-c2-Q3=25: 0.0625\n- DN40-c2-Q3=40: 0.100\n- DN50-c2-Q3=40: 0.100\n- DN50-c2-Q3=63: 0.1575\n- DN65-c2-Q3=63: 0.1575\n- DN65-c2-Q3=100: 0.250\n- DN80-c2-Q3=100: 0.25\n- DN80-c2-Q3=160: 0.40\n- DN100-c2-Q3=160: 0.40\n- DN100-c2-Q3=250: 0.625\n- DN125-c2-Q3=250: 0.625\n- DN125-c2-Q3=400: 1.00\n- DN150-c2-Q3=400: 1.00\n- DN150-c2-Q3=630: 1.575\n- DN200-c2-Q3=630: 1.575\n- DN250-c2-Q3=1000: 2.50\n- DN300-c2-Q3=1600: 4.00\n\n**transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h):**\n- DN65-c1: 0.64\n- DN80-c1-100: 1.00\n- DN80-c1-160: 1.02\n- DN100-c1-160: 1.6\n- DN100-c1-250: 1.6\n- DN125-c1-250: 2.50\n- DN125-c1-400: 2.56\n- DN150-c1-400: 4.00\n- DN150-c1-630: 4.03\n- DN200-c1-630: 6.30\n- DN250-c1-1000: 10\n- DN300-c1-1600: 16\n- DN25-c2-10: 0.040\n- DN25-c2-16: 0.064\n- DN40-c2-25: 0.100\n- DN40-c2-40: 0.160\n- DN50-c2-40: 0.160\n- DN50-c2-63: 0.252\n- DN65-c2-63: 0.250\n- DN65-c2-100: 0.400\n- DN80-c2-100: 0.40\n- DN80-c2-160: 0.64\n- DN100-c2-160: 0.64\n- DN100-c2-250: 1.00\n- DN125-c2-250: 1.00\n- DN125-c2-400: 1.60\n- DN150-c2-400: 1.60\n- DN150-c2-630: 2.52\n- DN200-c2-630: 2.52\n- DN250-c2-1000: 4.00\n- DN300-c2-1600: 6.40\n\n**permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h):**\n- DN65-c1: 100\n- DN80-c1-100: 100\n- DN80-c1-160: 160\n- DN100-c1-160: 160\n- DN100-c1-250: 250\n- DN125-c1-250: 250\n- DN125-c1-400: 400\n- DN150-c1-400: 400\n- DN150-c1-630: 630\n- DN200-c1-630: 630\n- DN250-c1-1000: 1000\n- DN300-c1-1600: 1600\n- DN25-c2-10: 10\n- DN25-c2-16: 16\n- DN40-c2-25: 25\n- DN40-c2-40: 40\n- DN50-c2-40: 40\n- DN50-c2-63: 63\n- DN65-c2-63: 63\n- DN65-c2-100: 100\n- DN80-c2-100: 100\n- DN80-c2-160: 160\n- DN100-c2-160: 160\n- DN100-c2-250: 250\n- DN125-c2-250: 250\n- DN125-c2-400: 400\n- DN150-c2-400: 400\n- DN150-c2-630: 630\n- DN200-c2-630: 630\n- DN250-c2-1000: 1000\n- DN300-c2-1600: 1600\n\n**overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h):**\n- DN65-c1: 125\n- DN80-c1-100: 125\n- DN80-c1-160: 200\n- DN100-c1-160: 200\n- DN100-c1-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c1-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c1-400: 500\n- DN150-c1-400: 500\n- DN150-c1-630: 787.5\n- DN200-c1-630: 787.5\n- DN250-c1-1000: 1250\n- DN300-c1-1600: 2000\n- DN25-c2-10: 12.5\n- DN25-c2-16: 20\n- DN40-c2-25: 31.3\n- DN40-c2-40: 50\n- DN50-c2-40: 50\n- DN50-c2-63: 78.75\n- DN65-c2-63: 78.75\n- DN65-c2-100: 125\n- DN80-c2-100: 125\n- DN80-c2-160: 200\n- DN100-c2-160: 200\n- DN100-c2-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c2-250: 312.5\n- DN125-c2-400: 500\n- DN150-c2-400: 500\n- DN150-c2-630: 787.5\n- DN200-c2-630: 787.5\n- DN250-c2-1000: 1250\n- DN300-c2-1600: 2000\n\n**ratio_q3_q1:**\nClass 1: 250, 160, 250, 160, 250, 160, 250, 160, 250, 160, 160, 160\nClass 2: all 400.\n\n**accuracy_class:** 1 for first 12, 2 for the rest.\n\n**maximum_admissible_pressure (bar):**\nClass 1: DN65:16, DN80-100:16, DN80-160:16, DN100-160:16, DN100-250:16, DN125-250:16, DN125-400:16, DN150-400:16, DN150-630:16, DN200-630:16, DN250-1000:10, DN300-1600:10.\nClass 2: DN25-10:16, DN25-16:16, DN40-25:16, DN40-40:16, DN50-40:16, DN50-63:16, DN65-63:16, DN65-100:16, DN80-100:16, DN80-160:16, DN100-160:16, DN100-250:16, DN125-250:16, DN125-400:16, DN150-400:16, DN150-630:16, DN200-630:16, DN250-1000:10, DN300-1600:10.\n\n**verification_scale_interval (m³):**\nClass 1: DN65:0.0001, DN80-100:0.0001, DN80-160:0.0001, DN100-160:0.001, DN100-250:0.001, DN125-250:0.001, DN125-400:0.001, DN150-400:0.001, DN150-630:0.001, DN200-630:0.001, DN250-1000:0.001, DN300-1600:0.01.\nClass 2: DN25-10:0.00001, DN25-16:0.00001, DN40-25:0.0001, DN40-40:0.0001, DN50-40:0.0001, DN50-63:0.0001, DN65-63:0.0001, DN65-100:0.0001, DN80-100:0.0001, DN80-160:0.0001, DN100-160:0.001, DN100-250:0.001, DN125-250:0.001, DN125-400:0.001, DN150-400:0.001, DN150-630:0.001, DN200-630:0.001, DN250-1000:0.001, DN300-1600:0.01.\n\nThis is going to be long but let's write it.\n\nShould I also include ratio_q3_q1 in model_level? Yes, dimensionless.\n\nNow matrix_tables — 5 tables. Let me name them:\n1. \"Metrological characteristics, accuracy class 1, DN65–DN125\" (page 3, table 1)\n2. \"Metrological characteristics, accuracy class 1, DN150–DN300\" (page 3, table 2)\n3. \"Metrological characteristics, accuracy class 2, DN25–DN65\" (page 4, table 1)\n4. \"Metrological characteristics, accuracy class 2, DN80–DN150\" (page 4, table 2)\n5. \"Metrological characteristics, accuracy class 2, DN200–DN300\" (page 5)\n\nColumns: [\"meter_size_variant\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max_admissible_pressure\", \"verification_scale_interval\"] with units [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"mm\", null, \"bar\", \"m³\"].\n\nHmm, the schema example used \"max_pressure_loss\" naming; I'll use \"max_admissible_pressure\". And add a \"model\" column linking to model_id? Could be helpful: first column \"model\" with model_id, plus meter_size. Let me include \"model\" and \"meter_size\". Actually keep simpler: include \"meter_size\" and rows have both q3 and ratio which distinguish variants. But to tie to model_family, include \"model\" column. I'll include both \"model\" and \"meter_size\".\n\nLet me draft rows for each table.\n\nTable 1 rows:\n1. {model: