certain → null. For bar → null. For V → maybe u:volt; the example lists \"V\" in unit_symbol examples but unit_id example only shows u:degree_Celsius. I'll set u:degree_Celsius for °C and null elsewhere.\n\n- indicating_range: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"C\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Battery\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- software_versions: {value: \"4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_\"}\n\nCertificate-level:\n- number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"SO13202564\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2013-07-16\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2006, amendment null, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [1, 2]. Should accuracy classes be strings? R49 classes are numeric 1 and 2 (unlike R60 C/D letters). I'll use [1, 2] as numbers... Hmm schema doesn't specify. Numbers seem fine; but to be safe maybe strings [\"1\",\"2\"]. The example in the YAML doesn't show. I'll use [\"1\",\"2\"]? Actually accuracy class 1/2 in water meters are typically written as numbers. I'll use [1, 2].\n\nTest reports: role \"type_evaluation_report\". Dates unknown → omit date field (don't fabricate). pages: 41 etc.\n\nNote the fourth report id has \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\" (typo in source). Rule: don't fabricate; but canonicalization? I'll keep as printed: \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\". Hmm, it's clearly a typo for R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01. I could normalize... I'll keep as printed to be faithful.\n\nRevision history: revision \"0\"? The table says \"Initial\" / 22 November 2011 / \"-\". Schema: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). For \"Initial\", revision: \"Initial\"? It's a string. I'll use \"Initial\" and changes: null. Second: \"1\", \"2013-07-16\", \"New software version and meter sizes added\".\n\nFootnotes: none → omit.\n\nAlso date_issued: the original was 22 Nov 2011, this revision issued 16 July 2013. date_issued: \"2013-07-16\".\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"Sharpflow\". The type is \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\". Family name could be \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\". I'll use \"Sharpflow\".\n\ncertified_type.type_designations: [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\", \"SWB7\", \"CWB7\"]? The type designation as stated is \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\". I'll include just \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\". Hmm, \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\" — SWB7 and CWB7 appear within the type string. I think listing [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\"] is cleanest; adding \"SWB7\" and \"CWB7\" separately is defensible. I'll add them too? The \"+\" joins the meter and the calculating/indicating device. To be safe and informative, I'll list [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\", \"SWB7\", \"CWB7\"]. Hmm, might overreach. I'll keep [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\"] only, since SWB7/CWB7 never appear alone as designations.\n\nNow write the JSON. Order of keys per schema: certificate, issuing_authority, applicants, manufacturers, certified_type, model_family, components (omit), characteristics, matrix_tables, recommendation, test_reports, revision_history, footnotes (omit).\n\nLet me double-check table 1 verification scale interval colspan: `<td>Verification scale interval (m³)</td><td colspan=\"3\">0,0001</td><td colspan=\"4\">0,001</td>` — 7 columns: first 3 (DN65, DN80×2) = 0.0001; last 4 (DN100×2, DN125×2) = 0.001. ✓.\n\nTable 2 (page 3 second): VSI colspan 4 = 0.001 (DN150×2, DN200, DN250), then 0.01 (DN300). Max pressure: colspan 3 = 16 (DN150×2, DN200), colspan 2 = 10 (DN250, DN300). ✓\n\nTable 3 (page 4 first): VSI colspan 2 = 0.00001 (DN25×2), colspan 6 = 0.0001. Max pressure 16 all. ✓\n\nTable 4 (page 4 second): VSI colspan 2 = 0.0001 (DN80×2), colspan 6 = 0.001. Max pressure 16 all. ✓\n\nTable 5 (page 5): Max pressure: DN200:16, DN250+DN300:10 (colspan 2). VSI: colspan 2 = 0.001 (DN200, DN250), DN300: 0.01. ✓\n\nCheck Q1 values class1 table2: DN150 col1: 2,50; col2: 2,52. DN200: 3,94. DN250: 6,25. DN300: 10. Q2: 4,00; 4,03; 6,30; 10; 16. ✓\n\nNow, write out model_level. I'll organize each attribute entry with all 31 models in consistent order (class 1 first, then class 2). For readability I'll keep the order same as models[] list.\n\nOne more consideration: the \"characteristics.model_level\" values shape — each value entry: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": <num>, ...unit at attribute level}. In the R137 example, unit_symbol is at the attribute level, and values just have {model, value}. I'll follow that: attribute-level unit_symbol, per-value {model, value}. Should each value also include unit_id? Example doesn't. Follow example.\n\nFor ratio_q3_q1 and accuracy_class: unit_symbol null.\n\nLet me now also decide whether to include \"components\". The type \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\": I'll add components? Rule 6: \"For complex instruments (e.g. 'Measuring sensor FM01 + Pulser P2 + Calculating device WB'), record each as a components[] entry\". The example mirrors our case: \"SWB7 + CWB7\". The description says \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\" — CWB7 is plausibly the calculating/indicating device and SWB7 the meter (measuring sensor + transducer). Given the R137-style guidance, recording components is encouraged for \"A + B\" types. Roles: SWB7 = \"water meter (measuring transducer/sensor body)\"? I must not fabricate roles not stated. The cert explicitly says \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\" — so role \"calculating/indicating device\" for CWB7 is supported-ish. And SWB7 would be \"water meter\" / \"measuring instrument\". Hmm.\n\nThe full type is \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\" where Sharpflow is the product family. I'll record components:\n- {role: \"Water meter (sensor)\", type_designations: [\"Sharpflow SWB7\", \"SWB7\"], ...} — the role \"sensor\" is a guess.\nRisky. Alternative: skip components. The cert identifies the certified type as a single water meter \"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\". I'll skip components to avoid fabrication. Actually... let me reconsider. The instruction example for components uses exactly this pattern with named roles. Here roles aren't named. I'll omit components — safer under rule 8.\n\nNow let me also think about whether temperature \"0,1/50\" — this means MAT range 0.1 °C to 50 °C. Convert to {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n\nLet me now write the final JSON.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"SO13202564\",\n    \"page_total\": 5,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2013-07-16\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"the Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"C. Oosterman\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Head Certification Board\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 78 6332332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Itron\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Eau et Energie Thermique\", \"11 Boulevard Pasteur\", \"67500 Haguenau\", \"France\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Itron\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Eau et Energie Thermique\", \"11 Boulevard Pasteur\", \"67500 Haguenau\", \"France\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Sharpflow SWB7 + CWB7\"],\n    \"description\": \"Water meter intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"Sharpflow\",\n    \"models\": [ ...31 entries... ]\n  },